<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pakubuwana I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1719">1719</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Pangeran Puger</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pakubuwana I, earlier Pangeran Puger, was ruler of Mataram from 1704 to 1719. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-i">Amangkurat I</a>, he claimed the throne against his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-iii">Amangkurat III</a> and won it with the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-javanese-war-of-succession">First Javanese War of Succession</a>. In return he ceded territory and trade rights on the north coast to the Company.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pakubuwana-i.jpg" alt="Pakubuwana I" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail_painting_of_Pangeran_Puger_(later_Pakubuwono_I)_forced_to_execute_his_daughter,_Raden_Ayu_Lembah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Raden Mas Darajat, he was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-i">Amangkurat I</a> by his second queen, Ratu Wetan, of the Kajoran family, descendants of the sultans of Pajang. When his father fell out with his half-brother Raden Mas Rahmat, Darajat was made crown prince, but the title was withdrawn in 1674 when the Kajoran family was shown to support the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya-rebellion">Trunajaya rebellion</a>. In 1677 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a> attacked the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plered">Plered</a>; Amangkurat I fled and Rahmat refused to defend the palace, so Puger stood against the rebels to prove his loyalty, before being forced back to Jenar, where he styled himself Susuhunan Ingalaga.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a> withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a>, Puger retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plered">Plered</a> and claimed the throne. Meanwhile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-i">Amangkurat I</a> had died in flight and Rahmat had become <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-ii">Amangkurat II</a>, crushing the rebellion with the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> by the end of 1679 and building a new court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartasura">Kartasura</a> in 1680. Puger refused to submit, and after a civil war he surrendered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> officer Jacob Couper on 28 November 1681 and accepted his half-brother's rule. When Captain François Tack came to Kartasura to arrest the fugitive Untung Suropati, Amangkurat II secretly sent Puger, disguised as one of Suropati's men, and in February 1686 Tack and his 75 soldiers were killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In May 1704 his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-iii">Amangkurat III</a> sent troops to destroy his family, but he escaped, and Rangga Yudanegara, regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semarang">Semarang</a>, persuaded the Company to back him in return for terms that included eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a>. Proclaimed Susuhunan Pakubuwana on 6 July 1704, he marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartasura">Kartasura</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, Semarang, Madurese and Surabaya troops and took it on 17 September 1705. A new treaty bound him to send 13,000 tons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> a year for 25 years.</p><p>Untung Suropati was killed at Bangil in 1706, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-iii">Amangkurat III</a> surrendered in 1708 and was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>. On the Company's evidence of treason he had Duke Jangrana II of Surabaya put to death; Surabaya then rebelled under Jayapuspita, who was defeated in 1718. He died in 1719, succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-iv">Amangkurat IV</a>, and most Javanese chronicles, written under his descendants, depict him as a wise ruler.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pakubuwono I of Mataram” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Surakarta, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartasura">Kartasura</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-7.57,110.82&amp;year=1709" aria-label="Surakarta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7615 2446 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pakubuwana I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8065 2716h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8065 2716h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-7.57,110.82&amp;year=1709">What was here in 1709? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
